#!/usr/bin/env -S mos build --open

#set document(
  title: "Author-Facing Line-Break Controls",
  author: "Mosaic contributors",
  language: "en",
)

#set page(
  paper: "A4",
  margin: 24mm,
)

= Three small contracts

Mosaic gives authors three knobs for line breaks: a non-breaking
space, a forced line break, and a soft hyphen. They are small
contracts. Each does one thing, and only one thing.

== Non-breaking space (U+00A0)

A literal U+00A0 between two words tells the line-breaker that
the pair must travel together. The greedy breaker honours this
because it splits only at ASCII whitespace, and U+00A0 is not
ASCII whitespace.

Authors who refuse to break "Mr. Smith" across a line write the
literal source `Mr.<NBSP>Smith` (where `<NBSP>` is one U+00A0
code point, two bytes in UTF-8). Rendered, the pair joins
as Mr. Smith, visually indistinguishable from a regular space,
but treated by the breaker as an indivisible unit. Likewise
`page<NBSP>4` keeps the label and number together, rendered as
page 4.

A single regular space, like the one between this word and the
next, is, of course, breakable like any other.

== Hard line break (`\\`)

The escape `\\` forces a line break inside a paragraph without
the extra leading that a blank-line paragraph break would emit.
Use it when the *line* is the unit of meaning: addresses, verse,
captions, the heroic-but-doomed code path where you really do
want this on one line\\and this on the next without spawning a
new paragraph.

Stack two of them\\\\and you get a blank line between two
otherwise-adjacent runs, still inside the same paragraph. The
paragraph's overall spacing-after is paid once, at the end.

A lone trailing `\` (no second backslash) is not an escape; it
emits the diagnostic W025 and falls through as a literal
backslash character.

== Soft hyphen (`\-` or U+00AD)

The soft hyphen marks a permitted-but-not-required break inside
a word. It is the author saying "if you ever need to break this
word, here is where to do it; otherwise pretend I was never
here". The shorthand `\-` expands to a literal U+00AD; the
codepoint itself works too.

Soft hyphens are *invisible when they aren't needed*: the codepoint
is stripped before shaping, so a word like
su\-per\-cali\-fra\-gil\-is\-tic\-ex\-pi\-al\-i\-do\-cious that
comfortably fits a line renders as supercalifragilisticexpialidocious
the same as if none of the `\-` shorthands had been written.

When a word *would* overflow the line, the greedy breaker now picks
the latest marked position whose prefix-plus-visible-hyphen still fits
the remaining space, emits `prefix-` at the end of the current line,
and continues with the suffix as the next word. If even the shortest
marked prefix can't fit an empty line, the existing oversize-cluster
fallback chops the word at glyph boundaries instead.

Optimal (non-greedy) selection across a whole paragraph is left for
the eventual Knuth-Plass line-breaker; the greedy slice here is the
smallest change that turns the SHY markers into a visible feature.
